{"status":"ok","message-type":"work","message-version":"1.0.0","message":{"indexed":{"date-parts":[[2026,8,1]],"date-time":"2026-08-01T01:34:03Z","timestamp":1785548043857,"version":"3.56.0"},"reference-count":51,"publisher":"American Physiological Society","issue":"1","funder":[{"DOI":"10.13039\/100008021","name":"Bristol Myers Squibb","doi-asserted-by":"crossref","id":[{"id":"10.13039\/100008021","id-type":"DOI","asserted-by":"crossref"}]}],"content-domain":{"domain":[],"crossmark-restriction":false},"short-container-title":["American Journal of Physiology-Gastrointestinal and Liver Physiology"],"published-print":{"date-parts":[[2019,1,1]]},"abstract":"<jats:p> Precision-cut liver tissue slice (PCLS) contains all major cell types of the liver parenchyma and preserves the original cell-cell and cell-matrix contacts. It represents a promising ex vivo model to study liver fibrosis and test the antifibrotic effect of experimental compounds in a physiological environment. In this study using RNA sequencing, we demonstrated that various pathways functionally related to fibrotic mechanisms were dysregulated in PCLSs derived from rats subjected to bile duct ligation. The activin receptor-like kinase-5 (Alk5) inhibitor SB525334, nintedanib, and sorafenib each reversed a subset of genes dysregulated in fibrotic PCLSs, and of those genes we identified 608 genes whose expression was reversed by all three compounds. These genes define a molecular signature characterizing many aspects of liver fibrosis pathology and its attenuation in the model. A panel of 12 genes and 4 secreted biomarkers including procollagen I, hyaluronic acid (HA), insulin-like growth factor binding protein 5 (IGFBP5), and WNT1-inducible signaling pathway protein 1 (WISP1) were further validated as efficacy end points for the evaluation of antifibrotic activity of experimental compounds. Finally, we showed that blockade of \u03b1<jats:sub>V<\/jats:sub>-integrins with a small molecule inhibitor attenuated the fibrotic phenotype in the model. Overall, our results suggest that the rat fibrotic PCLS model may represent a valuable system for target validation and determining the efficacy of experimental compounds. <\/jats:p><jats:p> NEW &amp; NOTEWORTHY We investigated the antifibrotic activity of three compounds, the activin receptor-like kinase-5 (Alk5) inhibitor SB525334, nintedanib, and sorafenib, in a rat fibrotic precision-cut liver tissue slice model using RNA sequencing analysis. A panel of 12 genes and 4 secreted biomarkers including procollagen I, hyaluronic acid (HA), insulin-like growth factor binding protein 5 (IGFBP5), and WNT1-inducible signaling pathway protein 1 (WISP1) were then established as efficacy end points to validate the antifibrotic activity of the \u03b1<jats:sub>V<\/jats:sub>-integrin inhibitor CWHM12. This study demonstrated the value of the rat fibrotic PCLS model for the evaluation of antifibrotic drugs. <\/jats:p>","DOI":"10.1152\/ajpgi.00281.2018","type":"journal-article","created":{"date-parts":[[2018,11,8]],"date-time":"2018-11-08T12:27:54Z","timestamp":1541680074000},"page":"G15-G24","source":"Crossref","is-referenced-by-count":12,"title":["Molecular characterization of a precision-cut rat liver slice model for the evaluation of antifibrotic compounds"],"prefix":"10.1152","volume":"316","author":[{"given":"Xinqiang","family":"Huang","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Bristol-Myers Squibb, Pennington, New Jersey"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Hong","family":"Cai","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Bristol-Myers Squibb, Pennington, New Jersey"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Ron","family":"Ammar","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Bristol-Myers Squibb, Pennington, New Jersey"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Yan","family":"Zhang","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Bristol-Myers Squibb, Pennington, New Jersey"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Yihe","family":"Wang","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Bristol-Myers Squibb, Pennington, New Jersey"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Kandasamy","family":"Ravi","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Bristol-Myers Squibb, Pennington, New Jersey"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"John","family":"Thompson","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Bristol-Myers Squibb, Pennington, New Jersey"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Gabor","family":"Jarai","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Bristol-Myers Squibb, Pennington, New Jersey"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"member":"24","reference":[{"key":"B1","doi-asserted-by":"publisher","DOI":"10.1038\/nrd3810"},{"key":"B2","doi-asserted-by":"publisher","DOI":"10.1155\/2015\/790203"},{"key":"B3","doi-asserted-by":"publisher","DOI":"10.3109\/08977194.2011.595714"},{"key":"B4","doi-asserted-by":"publisher","DOI":"10.1038\/labinvest.3700386"},{"key":"B5","doi-asserted-by":"publisher","DOI":"10.1007\/s00441-016-2407-9"},{"key":"B6","doi-asserted-by":"publisher","DOI":"10.1038\/sj.bjp.0706172"},{"key":"B7","doi-asserted-by":"publisher","DOI":"10.1016\/j.jaut.2013.07.008"},{"key":"B8","doi-asserted-by":"publisher","DOI":"10.1002\/path.2099"},{"key":"B9","doi-asserted-by":"publisher","DOI":"10.1111\/j.1478-3231.2005.01073.x"},{"key":"B10","doi-asserted-by":"publisher","DOI":"10.1053\/j.gastro.2008.03.003"},{"key":"B11","doi-asserted-by":"publisher","DOI":"10.1038\/nature02426"},{"key":"B12","doi-asserted-by":"publisher","DOI":"10.1007\/s00428-007-0390-y"},{"key":"B13","doi-asserted-by":"publisher","DOI":"10.1038\/nm.3282"},{"key":"B14","doi-asserted-by":"publisher","DOI":"10.1016\/j.yexmp.2006.12.003"},{"key":"B15","doi-asserted-by":"publisher","DOI":"10.1007\/s10620-012-2325-y"},{"key":"B16","doi-asser